WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:07.100
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from the CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:15.100
Today we are going to start with introduction to Verilog. So let us first introduce identifiers and keywords.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:25.100
Identifiers are designed defined names and they are used for constructs such as modules, input or output ports.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:32.100
They have to start with an uppercase or lowercase letter or an underscore.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:39.860
This one character can be followed by any number of letters, digits, underscores or

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:41.460
dollar signs.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:49.900
Beware, Verilog is case sensitive and these two variables where e is capital in this

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:56.200
one and en is capital in the second one are treated as two completely separate

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:02.920
variables. Alongside identifiers we do have keywords. They are part of language.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:10.260
They are words like module, input and output. These words are not allowed to be

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:16.660
used over identifiers. Complete list of keywords can be find in the chapter of

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:26.470
of xiBook listed in the bottom module is a basic building block of the language its syntax is such

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:36.630
that you first have word module and at the end you have word end module one word your compiler

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:48.070
shows all the sorry your editor in Rivado shows all the keywords in purple afterward module

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:55.610
you need to give a name for the module, so basically it's going to be module-identified,

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:59.690
and in the parentheses there is a set of input and output ports.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:04.330
This list can be empty or it can have multiple input and output.

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:07.850
Sometimes port can be also both input and output ports.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:15.530
So after keyword that determines if port is input or output, we have

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:24.810
size of the port if the port is different than only one bit as carry is here and then we have the

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:34.810
identifier for the input and output port. After the parentheses and semicolon the body of the

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:42.010
module is described here. The question is when does the module get to work? Most of the time I

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.370
I will actually use term,

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:47.690
when does the module get re-evaluated?

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:52.330
Well, it does get to be re-evaluated

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.570
if at least one of the inputs change its value.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.650
So let us look at the body of the module.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:02.050
In the body of the module,

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:06.290
we first present signal declarations

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.890
for all the internal signals in the module,

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.850
and that can be followed by any number

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:15.930
of continuous assignment statements, as shown here,

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:21.520
or module instantiations that will be introduced later.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.240
So signals, when they are declared a layer,

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.360
they have to first show the type, data type of the signal,

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.560
and they have to show the signal name in the end.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.840
If the signal width is different than zero,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.360
sorry, than one, then the range and the total,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.960
that implies the total size of the bits in the signal

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.000
is shown in between.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.320
You can declare one variable at a time,

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.520
or as shown here, you can declare multiple of that.

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:03.520
So this is both W0 and W1 are wires of the size of one bit.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.920
Signals are usually used for assignment statements,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.560
as shown here.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.840
but also they are used for module instantiations.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.180
Module instantiation is actually,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.920
module instantiation is actual,

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:25.080
for module instantiation we use it to connect modules

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.720
which are basically circuit parts

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.680
to each other using this virus.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.040
Important thing about signals is they do not keep values.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.960
So whatever is on the right hand side

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.980
And when this value changes,

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.440
the value of the left-hand side of the signal

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.900
gets re-evaluated and becomes a new value.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.300
We also need to know that by default,

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.960
all input and output ports are declared as wires.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:05:00.760
They can be redefined afterwards here,

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:04.160
but this needs to be redefined.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.640
This redefinition has to be explicit.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.180
So you basically, you have to explicitly say register

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.100
and so on and so forth.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.160
You will see those examples later.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.990
So after, inside of body of the module,

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:22.730
after signal declaration,

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:27.730
we can have some number of continuous assignment statements.

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.570
Continuous assignment statements

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:32.850
always start with keyword assign.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.490
And then on the left hand side,

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:43.450
there is a signal name followed by assignments operator and on the right hand side is expression.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:53.730
When will any of those assignments data be re-evaluated? Any time that any of the signals

00:05:53.730 --> 00:06:00.250
on the right hand side inside of expression changes its value, then that's when this

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:07.390
is going to be re-evaluated, kind of re-computed, and then the value of the signal itself on

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:14.970
the left hand side will potentially change its value. It is very important to understand

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:21.490
that these signal assignment statements, even though they are written one after another,

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:30.130
do not need to execute sequentially. They actually model parts of circuits and they

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:39.130
connections. And basically, in that scenario, by hybrid description language,

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:47.130
they do not need to execute in order. What does that mean? Suppose that value

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:54.930
of i and 0 changes. When value of i and 0 changes, this expression is going to be

00:06:54.930 --> 00:07:03.130
re-evaluated, a new value is going to be assigned to temp out. Out and carry get

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:11.090
to be assigned as portions of temp out. But we are not ever going to be certain

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:18.610
if this assignment statement or this assignment statement is going to be done

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:23.330
before each other. They are theoretically actually going to be

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:28.020
be executed at the very same moment.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.780
If you look at the right-hand side

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:32.280
of the continuous assignment statement,

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:38.660
usually what is placed there is an expression.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:41.860
The expressions are combination

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:45.180
of variables and operands.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.580
And for example, here in this example,

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:50.580
operand is plus for addition.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.180
But full list of operands,

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:59.340
one that is quite well organized can be found down here on the link before.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.830
Thank you.

